e unions but what about the  public anna subari  i think the public will be horrified by  this i think  that there's a recognition that our nhs  workers have gone way  beyond what anybody could have expected  of them  and the reality is is i mean i actually  think our nhs is  not just under terrible stress and  strain now and our work the workers  are absolutely exhausted but they are  that we are going in effect to need some  sort of  nhs recovery plan but  it's it's only in two of the papers and  it's the leaders you've identified  in the mirror and it's also the lead in  the guardian  it's not as far as i can see in or some  of the babies don't mention it at all  and i think that's quite deliberate  because i think that the public  will expect our nhs workers to get a lot  more  one percent it's just not good enough  yeah it's interesting isn't it and it's  kind of like it's slipped out as well  you know it's it's it there's a feeling  that it's been  sneaked out and that goes down really  badly  and dan hodges the royal college of  nursing have reacted calling this  pitiful and bitterly disappointing they  want to see a twelve and a half percent  increase  they say that one percent uh represents  three pounds fifty a week  extra for an experienced nurse now after  what those nurses have done for all of  us this year  bit of pill to swallow isn't it  it is but i think it's uh it's a  reflection of  of the broader issues that were thrown  up by the budget yesterday obviously  these are very bad headlines  um for rishi sunak we also saw some  some bad headlines yesterday in relation  to tax rises  i think we're going to see some bad  headlines moving forward in relation to  public spending cuts we're going to see  some bad headlines in relation to the  size  of the public debt and we're going to  see some bad headlines moving forward in  relation to the the quite anemic growth  that is predicted  once we've had the sort of post-coverage  v-shaped recovery  i think 